{"attack": true, "case": "The target's mechanism conflates 'corroboration' (having survived past severe tests) with 'corroborability'/'empirical content' (being highly testable). The claim defends preference for 'better-corroborated' theories, but the mechanism argues from 'higher empirical content (and thus higher corroborability)' and 'learning faster.' These are distinct Popperian concepts: a falsified theory can have high empirical content yet zero corroboration, while a trivially true tautology has low content. Popper's non-inductive, methodological defense straightforwardly applies to preferring high-content, highly-testable theories — but the target's claim is about preferring theories that have *survived* tests, i.e., corroboration. The 'maximizes exposure to falsification' rationale supports choosing the most testable conjecture, not the best-corroborated one. By substituting corroborability (a logical/a priori property) for corroboration (a historical, a posteriori property), the mechanism commits a non-sequitur: the argument from empirical content does not yield the conclusion about corroboration. The target's own countercondition (Tichý/Miller critique) hints at the fragility of the verisimilitude-to-corroboration link, but the deeper error is that the non-inductive defense the mechanism offers simply doesn't address the property the claim purports to defend.", "counterexample": [{"description": "Theory F has higher empirical content than Theory C and was severely tested and falsified; Theory C has lower empirical content and is well-corroborated (survived its tests). The target's mechanism says to choose higher empirical content 'to learn faster,' but the claim says to prefer better-corroborated theories. These directives conflict: F maximizes content/corroborability, C maximizes corroboration. The mechanism cannot defend the claim."}], "premise": "Corroboration (past survival of severe tests) and corroborability/empirical content (a priori testability) are distinct Popperian concepts; a non-inductive defense of the latter does not automatically defend the former.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "cd3ca5fef732e6786486dde4fbdb985dad2460e082a09fbe759a376263881266", "quote": "Choosing the theory with higher empirical content (and thus higher corroborability) is the only way to learn faster, even if it doesn't make the theory more likely to be true."}], "successor_question": "Can the target supply a non-inductive mechanism that specifically defends preference for corroboration (past test-survival) rather than merely corroborability (testability), without smuggling in an expectation that past survival predicts future performance?"}